        "<a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/Gaurav4776608/Data-Cleaning-Processing-and-EDA/blob/main/Handling%20Missing%20Values/Part%201\" target=\"_parent\"><img src=\"https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg\" alt=\"Open In Colab\"/></a>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "What are missing values?\n",
        "In a given dataset most of the times it will happen that there are NaN values or blank spaces sometimes often treated as null values. So if we train our model on this tyoe of dataset we might get an error or very low accuracy. So we need to handle this kind of situation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two ways to handle missing values:-\n",
        "There are two ways which we can be used to handle missing values\n",
        "1. Removal of missing values\n",
        "2. Imputers"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "UQxn_uLI5Lwx"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Removal of Missing Values\n",
        "\n",
        "Here we can drop a particular row or data.\n",
        "For example, we have a dataframe of 5x4 size. On (3,1) means third row of the first attribute have a null value then to handle it we can remove that particular row. We can use .dropna() function to handle this.\n",
        "\n",
        "Although its not that much recommended as if we are removing a full row that means in here we are removing all the rest of the column's data like in here we are removing (3, 2), (3, 3), (3, 4) also which might hold some very crucial data."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0CGa1SAy57xI"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Imputer\n",
        "\n",
        "Imputer means we will be filling all those places which contain a  missing values instead of removing the whole row.\n",
        "\n",
        "Imputer can be divided into 2 parts\n",
        "1. Univariate\n",
        "2. Multivariate"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "wmd2-CVT7Zio"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Univariate\n",
        "\n",
        "Univariate means here we will dealing with only 1 column or attribute.\n",
        "\n",
        "Univariate columns can be of two types. First is numerical and second is categorical.\n",
        "\n",
        "Numerical attribute's values can be handled with methods like:\n",
        "1. Mean/Median\n",
        "2. Filling with random values\n",
        "3. End of distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "Categorical attribute's values can be handled with methods like:\n",
        "1. Mode\n",
        "2. \"Missing\" this can be written inside those places\n",
        "\n",
        "These all things can be handled using Simple Imputer Class"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "q36OBJxF7rg5"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Multivariate\n",
        "Multivariate means here we will be dealing with more than 1 column or attribute.\n",
        "Multivariate columns can be handled with two types of imputers:\n",
        "1. KNN Imputer\n",
        "2. Iterative Imputer (this uses an algorith called MICE)"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YD3Grgm28W_A"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "What is CCA?\n",
        "\n",
        "CCA i.e. Complete Case Analysis is a process of discarding any rows in a dataset where the column value is missing. In simple words we can say that removal of rows where missing values exist that process is called CCA.\n",
        "\n",
        "Advantages:\n",
        "The only advantage is its very easy to handle.\n",
        "\n",
        "Disadvantages:\n",
        "1. Data distribution can be affected\n",
        "2. Model when in production, it will not know how to handle missing values\n",
        "3. May lead to removal of some important data which was in the removed row\n",
        "\n",
        "When to use?\n",
        "\n",
        "Its said that CCA should be done only when the missing data is in random order, there should not be any particular sequence of missing data (MCAR).\n",
        "The missing values should be less than or equal to 5% of the overall data means if there are 1000 rows and for them if missing values row is less than 50 then only we should apply CCA"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "LyjPLCvI9TBo"
      }
    },
